Horst – Background Story

Horst was given to the Church as a child, not out of cruelty, but out of necessity. It was not uncommon—too many mouths, too few resources. The Church raised him, fed him, educated him. For that alone, it earned a kind of loyalty that faith never could.

He never became a true believer. To Horst, the gods were… inconclusive. Distant, inconsistent, or perhaps simply irrelevant. What mattered was that the Church worked. It provided structure, protection, and a way to deal with things no one else could—or would.

That didn’t mean it was good.

Horst grew up seeing the cracks: quiet corruption, buried reports, decisions made for convenience rather than truth. Over time, he came to see the Church not as holy, but as necessary—a flawed tool holding worse chaos at bay.

He found his place not in prayer, but in investigation.

While others devoted themselves to ritual, Horst solved problems. Missing persons, strange sightings, rumors of curses—most of it nonsense. Fear, superstition, or people wanting to be heard. The Church understood this, even if it never said so openly.

So they sent Horst.

Not an Inquisitor. Not officially.

Horst became what some within the institution quietly referred to as a “second-class fire extinguisher”—the man you send when a situation is inconvenient, unclear, or not worth acknowledging. If people needed reassurance, he would give it. If something needed to disappear, he would handle it. And if the report ended up unread in some archive, all the better.

His job was not to find the truth.

His job was to resolve things.

That changed when he decided to break routine.

The hours of prayer had always felt empty to him, so one day, out of boredom more than rebellion, Horst experimented with mushrooms grown in the monastery gardens. What followed was not insight—but contact.

Something saw him.

Or perhaps, something had always been watching and finally chose to answer.

The entity did not explain itself. It did not demand worship. It simply spoke—in fragments, impressions, glimpses that felt more like being observed than addressed. When Horst came back to his senses, something had changed.

He had power.

Since then, the line between illusion and reality has grown increasingly thin. Horst experiences visions—sometimes subtle, sometimes deeply unsettling. To steady himself, he began using herbs and smoke from the monastery gardens. Whether this truly helps or merely dulls the edge is unclear.

He continues his work.

Pragmatic as ever, Horst does not concern himself with questions he cannot answer. The supernatural exists. That much is no longer debatable. Whether it is divine, infernal, or something else entirely is irrelevant.

You deal with what is in front of you.

The Church still sends him where things are uncertain, inconvenient, or better left unexamined.

And Horst goes.

Out of loyalty. Out of habit.

Or perhaps because, deep down, he understands that whatever is speaking to him seems most interested in exactly those kinds of cases.
